{"qid":"Q4962","names":["Georgia"],"title":"Georgia","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Kingdom of Georgia reached its medieval zenith under Queen Tamar (r. 1184–1213), controlling the South Caucasus, parts of northeastern Anatolia, and the Pontic coast, and hosting a court patronizing Georgian literature and ecclesiastical architecture. Mongol invasions from 1220 onward fractured the realm into competing Georgian and Armenian sub-kingdoms, though a partial revival occurred under George V the Brilliant (r. 1314–1346). Fragmentation into the kingdoms of Kartli, Kakheti, and Imereti persisted through the early modern period, with repeated Ottoman and Safavid interventions; Georgia was absorbed into the Russian Empire between 1801 and 1810, then briefly independent (1918–1921) before Soviet annexation and final restoration of independence in 1991.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Georgia's territorial extent varied enormously across its 567–2025 attestation span; the medieval kingdom's core is well mapped, but peripheral control during fragmentation and early modern periods is subject to significant uncertainty.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: History of Georgia (country)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Georgia_(country)"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Kingdom of Georgia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Georgia"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Tamar the Great","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tamar_the_Great"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Georgia","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Guaram I","nativeName":"გუარამ I","roman":"Guaram I","start":588,"end":590,"image":nu